WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:28.769
Mood naturally fluctuates and everyone experiences highs and lows in their lives

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:32.939
But in some cases changes in mood can become long-lasting

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:39.150
Debilitating and can impair someone's ability to hold down a job, or sustain meaningful relationships

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:46.259
It's estimated that around 10 percent of people will at some point in their lives suffer from depression a mood disorder

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.690
characterized by several symptoms

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.449
feeling sad

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.940
Distressed

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.160
Unmotivated

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.360
Excessively tired

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:03.029
And losing interest in once pleasurable activities known as anhedonia

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:06.779
Many people with depression also suffer from anxiety

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:16.229
Neuroimaging studies reveal that many brain circuits that normally regulate mood are dysregulated in depression

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:24.419
Located deep within the brain the amygdala processes highly salient stimuli such as rewards and potential threats

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:30.570
In depression the amygdala is overactive and responds excessively to negative events

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:37.350
In turn the amygdala connects to a set of brain regions that hone the physiological and behavioral

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:44.400
Response to emotional stimuli these areas include the medial prefrontal cortex the nucleus accumbens

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.780
the hippocampus and the

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.780
insula

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:56.730
The hippocampus is involved in memory formation and along with the prefrontal cortex is particularly vulnerable to the effects of stress

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:05.039
Depressed people are more susceptible to stress which can cause physical changes in the brain including atrophy of the hippocampus

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:12.089
This and other changes in depressed people may cause inappropriate responses to emotional events

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:18.589
The medial prefrontal cortex is involved in regulating how strongly we react to emotional stimuli

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:25.429
Treatments such as antidepressant drugs cognitive behavioral therapy and electroconvulsive therapy

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.509
affect the structure and function of these and other brain regions

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.819
Animal models such as mice are critical in helping us understand the cellular and molecular

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.510
changes underlying depression and develop better treatments

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.469
Although it's impossible to know if a mouse is depressed

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:50.719
My subjected to chronic stress show some symptoms similar to depressed humans

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:57.740
Such as anxiety like behavior less social interaction and a lack of interest in normally pleasurable activities

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:05.660
While not all human depression is triggered by stress these models may still shed light on the biology of depression

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.290
and they're the closest scientists can get while studying mice as

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:15.619
With humans chronic stress in mice can lead to atrophy of the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:22.999
Mouse studies have also shown altered neuronal plasticity in various brain regions including the hippocampus

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.210
prefrontal cortex amygdala and nucleus accumbens

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.499
In a healthy hippocampus

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:38.570
Experiences can lead to changes in the connections between neurons resulting in learning these changes are referred to as plasticity

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.540
chronic stress can reduce this plasticity

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.740
Healthy brains also continue to produce new neurons in one part of the hippocampus

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:56.990
These new neurons slowly mature and integrate into the circuitry where they have a strong effect on hippocampal activity and behavior

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:03.229
These new neurons are also affected by stress. They reduced in number in stressed brains

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:11.420
These effects may result from reduced levels of neurotrophins proteins that increase neuronal growth and plasticity

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.960
Reduced plasticity may stop the hippocampus from being able to properly regulate the stress response

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:22.309
Which may lead to a vicious cycle where stress perpetuates more stress

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.370
The hippocampus is particularly affected

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.649
But there can be reductions in plasticity

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:34.150
elsewhere in the brain and together these changes could contribute to other symptoms of depression like an dona

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:39.640
Whether these cellular changes seen in mice are involved in human depression remains unclear

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:47.020
Most antidepressants available today rapidly increase the amount of the neurotransmitters serotonin

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.050
and/or norepinephrine in the synapse

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:58.210
However improvements in symptoms in patients and mice usually don't occur until weeks after starting the treatment

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:01.509
While the reasons for this delay aren't fully understood

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:08.409
Prolonged treatment with antidepressants can over time act to reverse some of the changes induced by chronic stress

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.760
increasing neurotrophins expression and rebooting hippocampal plasticity

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:21.220
Non-chemical treatments for depression including electroconvulsive shock also promote, hippocampal plasticity in mice

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:28.869
Antidepressant treatment can also reverse stress induced changes in other areas of the brain including the prefrontal cortex

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:31.240
and reward circuitry

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.809
different treatments may target different regions to improve symptoms

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:39.580
Recently the drug ketamine was found to have rapid antidepressant

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:46.330
Effects in patients with depression as well as in rodent models with effects lasting for days the mechanism behind

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.330
This is an area of active research

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.239
Ketamine blocks a type of synaptic transmission

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:58.390
Leading to activation of a number of signaling pathways an increasing neurotrophins expression

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:05.110
these molecular changes result in increased plasticity in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus and

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.080
likely contribute to ketamine's behavioural effects

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:14.329
By studying the changes in the brain caused by chronic stress

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.750
And how antidepressants like ketamine work to reverse them?

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:22.669
Researchers may find new targets for treatment or new drugs that could act more quickly

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:27.199
More specifically or more effectively than currently available treatments

